RetroShader Lab — third-party notice for public/shaders
=======================================================

This directory has two upstreams. Nothing here is modified: every file is a
byte-identical copy, so what the lab compiles is exactly what the device does.

NextUI  (GPL-3.0)
-----------------
    https://github.com/LoveRetro/NextUI
    Licensed under the GNU General Public License v3.0.
    See LICENSE in this directory for the full text.

    glsl/         21 shaders copied verbatim from  skeleton/BASE/Shaders/glsl/
    default.glsl  copied verbatim from  skeleton/SYSTEM/desktop/shaders/default.glsl
                  (the final scale pass of the minarch pipeline)
    presets/      copied verbatim from  skeleton/BASE/Shaders/
                  (the stock shader configs, including the per-system sets/)

perfect-retroshaders  (MIT)
---------------------------
    https://github.com/sinedied/perfect-retroshaders
    Copyright (c) 2026 sinedied, MIT — the full text is in each file's header.

    glsl/   crt-perfect.glsl, crt-perfect-v2.glsl, crt-perfect-v3.glsl,
            crt-perfect-v4.glsl, crt-perfect-v5.glsl, crt-perfect-v5b.glsl,
            lcd-perfect.glsl, pixel-perfect.glsl

    These were previously carried by NextUI and released into the public domain;
    they are now maintained in their own repository under MIT. NextUI no longer
    ships them, so this project tracks them from upstream directly.

    Their presets in presets/ (crt-perfect*.cfg, lcd-perfect.cfg,
    pixel-perfect.cfg) are written for this project rather than copied, since the
    upstream repository does not carry minarch configs. Each uses the pass
    settings its shader's header requires: a NEAREST sampler and rendering at the
    final output resolution.

Shader sources
--------------
Most of the NextUI shaders originate from the libretro GLSL shader collection
(https://github.com/libretro/glsl-shaders) and each file keeps the license
stated in its own header. As shipped here, that is:

    GNU General Public License (v2 or later, per the file header):
        barrel-distortion.glsl, bicubic.glsl, fast-sharpen.glsl,
        pixel_art_AA.glsl, scale3x.glsl

    MIT (see perfect-retroshaders above):
        crt-perfect.glsl, crt-perfect-v2.glsl, crt-perfect-v3.glsl,
        crt-perfect-v4.glsl, crt-perfect-v5.glsl, crt-perfect-v5b.glsl,
        lcd-perfect.glsl, pixel-perfect.glsl

    Public domain:
        image-adjustment.glsl, lcd1x.glsl, res-independent-scanlines.glsl,
        sharp-bilinear.glsl, sharp-shimmerless.glsl,
        sharp-shimmerless-grid.glsl, sharp-shimmerless-subpixel-vrgb.glsl

    No explicit header — see the upstream libretro repository and the
    copyright lines inside each file:
        aa-shader-4.0.glsl, dmg_dot_matrix.glsl, edge1pixel.glsl,
        lcd3x.glsl, pixellate.glsl, retro-v2.glsl, scanline.glsl,
        stock.glsl, waterpaint.glsl

Individual copyright holders named in the shader headers include, among
others, Team XBMC, Andrea Mazzoleni, guest(r), Fes, Gigaherz, jdgleaver and
the libretro contributors.

MIT is compatible with this project's GPL-3.0-or-later licensing: the
combined work remains GPL-3.0-or-later, and the MIT files keep their own
notice, which their headers carry verbatim.
